  "prediction": "**The Holman Rule: A Summary of Congressional History and Precedents**\n\nThe Holman Rule is a congressional rule that separates policy questions from funding decisions in the House of Representatives. Established in 1835, the rule prohibits the inclusion of legislative provisions in appropriation bills unless they \"retrench expenditures\" by reducing the number and salary of federal officials, the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury, or the amounts of money covered in an appropriation bill.\n\nOver the years, the rule has undergone several changes, with the most significant revision occurring in 1983. However, in 2017, the House reinstated a broader version of the rule, which allows for retrenchments resulting from a reduction of the number and salary of the officers of the United States or the reduction of the compensation of any person paid out of the Treasury of the United States.\n\nThe rule is intended to apply only when an obvious reduction of funds in a general appropriations bill is achieved by the provision in question. Precedents have established that the reduction must be specific, not contingent on an event, and must apply to funds other than those appropriated in the pending general appropriations bill.\n\nThe Holman Rule has been used to allow consideration of amendments that reduce expenditures, such as proposals to limit the number of employees, reduce salaries, or consolidate offices. However, the rule does not allow for broad legislative provisions that would expand the scope of the bill or apply to funds other than those appropriated in the pending general appropriations bill.\n\nIn the 115th Congress, the House considered several amendments under the Holman Rule, including one that would have reduced the salary of a federal administrator and another that would have abolished a division of the Congressional Budget Office. While these amendments failed to pass, they demonstrate the potential impact of the Holman Rule on congressional decision-making.\n\nIn conclusion, the Holman Rule is a complex and nuanced congressional rule that has evolved over time. Its application is guided by precedents and is intended to promote fiscal responsibility by separating policy questions from funding decisions.",
